    "dc.description.abstract" : [ {
      "value" : "Summary of the Copyright Act, 1957\n\nKey Provisions:\n1.\tWorks Protected by Copyright \no\tThe Act provides copyright protection for: \n\tLiterary works. \n\tDramatic works. \n\tMusical works. \n\tArtistic works. \n\tCinematograph films. \n\tSound recordings. \n\tComputer programs, databases, and other works covered through subsequent amendments. \n2.\tOwnership and Duration of Copyright \no\tCopyright generally vests in the author or creator of the work, subject to certain exceptions. \no\tThe Act specifies the duration of copyright protection for different categories of works. \n3.\tRights of Copyright Owners \no\tCopyright owners enjoy exclusive rights to: \n\tReproduce the work. \n\tPublish, distribute, and communicate the work to the public. \n\tPerform or adapt the work. \n\tTranslate or license the work. \n\tAuthorise others to exercise these rights. \n4.\tAssignment and Licensing \no\tCopyright may be assigned or licensed wholly or partly through written agreements. \no\tThe Act lays down the conditions governing assignment and licensing of copyright. \n5.\tRegistration of Copyright \no\tThe Act provides for the registration of copyright through the Copyright Office. \no\tRegistration is not mandatory for the existence of copyright but serves as prima facie evidence of ownership. \n6.\tCopyright Infringement \no\tThe Act defines acts constituting copyright infringement. \no\tIt provides civil and criminal remedies, including injunctions, damages, seizure of infringing copies, fines, and imprisonment. \n7.\tExceptions and Fair Dealing \no\tThe Act permits certain uses of copyrighted works without the owner's permission under the doctrine of fair dealing, including: \n\tPrivate or personal use. \n\tResearch and study. \n\tCriticism or review. \n\tReporting of current events. \n\tEducational and other specified purposes. \n8.\tCopyright Board and Appellate Mechanism \no\tThe Act originally provided for the establishment of the Copyright Board to decide specified disputes. \no\tFollowing later amendments, its functions have been transferred to other authorities, including the Commercial Courts and the Intellectual Property Division of the High Courts, as applicable.",
      "language" : null,
      "authority" : null,
      "confidence" : -1,
      "place" : 0
    } ],
